    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 171855\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n1155 AM PDT Sat Oct 17 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* Light rain is possible Saturday afternoon into Sunday, but rain \n  impacts are not likely through the weekend. \n\n* Precipitation chances and winds return to the region early to\n  middle of next week, with significant uncertainty in forecast\n  and impact details.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...Through next Friday\n\nRadar and satellite imagery through the night showed a decently \norganized circulation over southeast California/southwest Arizona\nas rain and lightning developed. This is likely associated with \na piece of energy that was cut off and left behind from the \nshortwave the past few days. Hi-res models have been slow to pick \nup this feature and what it will do the next 36 hours. Surface \nanalysis showed around 1.00 PWATs with this feature that were \nslowly creeping north. HREF has a decent handle on this and shows \nPWATs increase to over 0.75 inches by Sunday morning through the \nColorado River Valley and western Arizona. Modest moisture \ncombined with vorticity advection rotating around the center of \nthe low should be enough to light rain to develop at times late \nSaturday afternoon into Sunday. Unsure how much will reach the \nground, and probabilities for impactful rainfall amounts is low- \nso likely will not see rainfall impacts with this round of \nprecipitation. If better rainfall were to develop, it would be in \nthe terrain of Mohave and Clark counties as easterly upslope flow \ncould enhance light rain. 90th percentile \"worst case scenario\" \n24hr rainfall amounts show about 0.50 inches of rain in these \nareas, so high impact weather is not expected. Given the models \nseem behind on picking up on this piece of energy and what it will\ndo, mesoanalysis late morning and early afternoon will paint a \nbetter picture of what may occur and adjustments to precipitation \nchances are possible. Otherwise, winds should remain light as \ntemperatures drop to slightly below normal. \n\nAfter this first system lifts out and is absorbed into the mean\nflow, another more potent trough is expected to impact the \nregion Tuesday through much of the work week. On Tuesday as the \ndeepening trough approaches, breezy south to southwest winds \ndevelop. Probabilities for impactful wind gusts over 40 MPH have \ndropped and generally remain below 50%, so wind impacts are \nbecoming less of a concern. With strong forcing associated with \nthe cutoff low and moisture in place over the region, \nprecipitation chances are possible starting Tuesday and continuing\nta times through the week. Current forecast has chances peaking \nWednesday into Thursday with all areas seeing at least a 30% \nchance for precipitation. Highest chances and precipitation \namounts will be in Inyo County through the Southern Great Basin \nwhere the strongest forcing is currently expected to set up. \nAccumulating snow is also likely, mainly above 9000ft. All that \nsaid- there continues to be significant uncertainty in the track \nand strength of the system as it moves inland, as well as \ndiscrepancies in how much moisture will be available. Increasing \nconfidence that there will be precipitation next week, especially \nWednesday and Thursday, but continued uncertainty in exact details\nand impacts as model disagreement persists. With increasing cloud\ncover and precipitation chances, temperatures will remain below \nnormal next week.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 18Z Forecast Package...Cloud \ncover continues to build in from the south with scattered clouds \naround 7000 ft AGL affecting the site this afternoon trough tomorrow \nafternoon. Non-impactful showers will be possible this afternoon and \ninto the night, with another wave of non-impactful rain moving \nthrough the area in the mid morning to mid afternoon on Sunday. \nWinds will be below 10 kts through the TAF period. Expect \nnortheasterly winds into the late afternoon before turning due \neast. Winds turn southeasterly into the early night and become light \nand variable into the late night. Winds become easterly again Sunday \nafternoon.\n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast \nCalifornia...For the 18Z Forecast Package...Light shower activity is \nbuilding into the region from the south, but any rainfall looks to \nbe non-impactful and fails to reach KBIH nor KDAG. Showers cut off \ninto the evening and early night, but a secondary batch arrives \novernight and into Sunday morning. Winds remain mostly light \nthrough the TAF period.\n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Nickerson\nAVIATION...Murdock\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
